1. Why is the redirect_uri parameter one of the most dangerous parts of OAuth?
	If OAuth authentication happens between the user, the authorization server, and the application, why is the redirect_uri parameter such a common attack vector?
Ans:- To answer this well, you must understand the OAuth authorization code flow.
Step-by-Step OAuth Flow:

Imagine a user logging into an app using OAuth 2.0 with OpenID Connect.
The login starts like this:
User Browser
     │
     ▼
Application
     │
     ▼
Authorization Server
The application redirects the user to the authorization server.
Example request:
https://auth.example.com/authorize?
client_id=123
&redirect_uri=https://app.example.com/callback
&response_type=code
&scope=openid

Here the redirect_uri tells the authorization server where to send the authorization code after login.
After the user authenticates:
Authorization Server
        │
        ▼
redirect_uri?code=AUTH_CODE
The code is sent back to the application.

-> Where the Problem Appears:
If the authorization server does not strictly validate redirect_uri, an attacker can manipulate it.
Example attack request:
https://auth.example.com/authorize?
client_id=123
&redirect_uri=https://evil.com/callback
Now the authorization server will redirect the user to the attacker:
https://evil.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
The attacker receives the authorization code and exchanges it for tokens.
Now the attacker logs in as the victim.

-> Why This Happens:-
Many developers validate redirect URIs poorly.
Common mistakes:
• allowing wildcard domains
• using substring matching
• accepting any URL with the same domain

Example vulnerable check:
if redirect_uri contains "app.example.com"
Attacker bypass:
https://app.example.com.evil.com/callback

-> Correct Mitigation:
The authorization server must enforce:
• Exact redirect_uri match
• The redirect URI must be pre-registered and compared exactly.
• Example secure approach:
Registered:
https://app.example.com/callback
Allowed:
✔ exact match
Rejected:
✘ https://app.example.com.evil.com
✘ https://evil.com

-> The Key Insight:
Redirect URI validation protects authorization codes.
If this validation fails, the entire OAuth flow collapses.

2. Why is the state parameter critical in OAuth?
Ans:- The OAuth Login Flow:
When an application sends the user to the authorization server:
GET /authorize?
client_id=app123
&redirect_uri=https://app.example.com/callback
&state=xyz123
The state value is generated by the application.
It is stored in the user's session.
After authentication, the authorization server redirects:
https://app.example.com/callback?
code=AUTH_CODE
&state=xyz123
The application checks if the returned state matches the stored value.

-> What Happens If state Is Missing?
Without the state parameter, OAuth becomes vulnerable to login CSRF.
Attack scenario:
Attacker logs into the app with their own account.
They obtain:
https://app.example.com/callback?code=ATTACKER_CODE
They trick the victim into visiting that URL.
Now the victim's browser completes the login using the attacker's authorization code.
The victim unknowingly logs into the attacker's account.

-> Why This Is Dangerous:-
This sounds harmless, but it enables serious attacks.
Example:
• Attacker account contains malicious data.
• Victim uploads confidential files thinking it's their account.
• But everything goes to the attacker.

-> Correct Mitigation:
The application must verify:
returned_state == stored_state
The state should be:
• random
• unpredictable
• stored in session

-> Key Insight:
The state parameter prevents cross-site request forgery in OAuth flows.

3. Why is the Implicit Flow considered insecure today?
Older OAuth systems used implicit flow.
Example:
response_type=token

Instead of returning an authorization code, the authorization server returns the token directly.
https://app.example.com/callback#access_token=TOKEN

The token appears in the URL fragment.

-> Why This Was Designed:
Implicit flow was created for JavaScript applications that couldn't safely store client secrets.

-> Why It Became Dangerous:-
Tokens exposed in URLs create several leak points.
Example:
https://app.example.com/#access_token=XYZ
This token can leak through:
Browser history
Referrer headers
Logs
Malicious JavaScript
Third-party scripts

-> Real Attack Scenario:
•  User logs into an application.
•  The page loads third-party analytics:
<script src="analytics.js">
•  If analytics logs URLs, the token gets captured.

-> Modern Solution:
OAuth now recommends:
Authorization Code Flow + PKCE

PKCE prevents code interception.
Flow:
Browser
   │
   ▼
Authorization Server
   │
   ▼
Return Authorization Code
   │
   ▼
App exchanges code with PKCE verifier

Even if code is stolen, attacker cannot exchange it.

-> Key Insight:-
Implicit flow was replaced because tokens should never travel in browser URLs.

4. Why is ID Token validation often implemented incorrectly?
OIDC adds identity on top of OAuth 2.0.
The identity information comes in an ID Token, which is a JSON Web Token.
Example ID token:
header.payload.signature

Payload:
{
 "iss": "https://auth.example.com",
 "aud": "client123",
 "exp": 1712345678,
 "sub": "user123"
}

-> Common Developer Mistake:-
Developers only decode the JWT.
Example:
const user = decode(id_token)
They trust the payload.
But they never verify the signature.

-> Attack Scenario:-
Attacker creates fake token:
{
 "sub": "admin",
 "aud": "client123"
}
Signs it with their own key.
If the app does not verify the signature, the attacker becomes admin.

-> Correct Token Validation:-
Applications must validate:
signature
issuer
audience
expiration
nonce

Example checks:
iss == trusted issuer
aud == application client id
exp > current time

Key Insight:- 
JWT decoding ≠ JWT validation.
The signature verification is what makes the token trustworthy.
